 "capsule": "The Ghana Empire, known to its own Soninke rulers as Wagadu (the title 'ghana' meaning war-chief was applied by Arab writers to its kings), dominated the western Sahel from its capital, most likely Koumbi Saleh, controlling the trans-Saharan trade in gold from the Bambuk and Bure fields exchanged for Saharan salt. Arab geographers from the eighth century onward, such as al-Fazari and later al-Bakri in the eleventh century, describe Ghana as fabulously wealthy, its king able to field large cavalry armies and exact tribute from surrounding Sahelian peoples along the upper Senegal and Niger. Weakened by Almoravid pressure from the north in the later eleventh century and by the exhaustion of easily worked gold deposits, Ghana's authority fragmented through the twelfth century, and by 1203 the Sosso ruler Sumanguru Kante had eclipsed it, opening the way for its eventual absorption into the rising Mali Empire.",
